When building a web application that handles user‑supplied data, you should sanitize and validate input at every boundary of your stack. Below is a practical, technology‑specific checklist for the four frameworks you mentioned.

| Layer | Technology | Sanitization / Validation Technique | Why it matters |
|-------|------------|-------------------------------------|----------------|
| **Client‑side (React)** | • Use **controlled components** and **type‑safe props**.<br>• Leverage libraries like **DOMPurify** or **sanitize-html** for any content that will be rendered as HTML.<br>• Enforce **form field constraints** (`minLength`, `maxLength`, `pattern`) in the UI. | Prevents accidental injection of malicious markup or oversized payloads before the data leaves the browser. |
| **Client‑side (React)** | • **React Helmet** or **content‑security‑policy** headers to restrict inline scripts. | Reduces risk of XSS if a user manages to inject code that bypasses the UI. |
| **Server‑side (FastAPI)** | • Define **Pydantic models** with explicit field types, regex patterns, and `conint`, `constr`, `EmailStr`, etc.<br>• Use `@validator` methods for custom sanitization (e.g., stripping tags, normalizing whitespace).<br>• Set `orm_mode = True` only when you’re sure the data originates from a trusted source. | Pydantic validates data before it reaches your business logic, ensuring type safety and basic content constraints. |
| **Server‑side (FastAPI)** | • Use **dependency injection** to apply **rate limiting** or **CAPTCHA** checks on endpoints that accept user input.<br>• Employ **SQLAlchemy** with parameterized queries or ORM methods to avoid SQL injection. | Keeps the database layer safe from injection attacks. |
| **Server‑side (Spring)** | • Annotate request payloads with **`@Valid`** and use **Hibernate Validator** constraints (`@NotNull`, `@Size`, `@Pattern`, `@Email`).<br>• For JSON fields that may contain HTML, apply **JSoup** sanitization (`Jsoup.clean`) before persisting or rendering.<br>• Use **`@CrossOrigin`** with a strict whitelist to mitigate CSRF. | Spring’s validation framework guarantees that only well‑formed data enters your services. |
| **Server‑side (Spring)** | • Configure **Spring Security** with **CSRF protection** and **XSS protection headers** (`X-XSS-Protection`, `Content-Security-Policy`). | Adds a defensive layer against common web attacks. |
| **Database** | • Store sanitized data only. Use **prepared statements** everywhere. | Even if the application layer is secure, the database should not be the last line of defense. |
| **General** | • Keep all dependencies up to date and monitor for security advisories.<br>• Log validation failures and reject requests with clear error messages (avoid leaking stack traces). | Maintains overall application hygiene and aids in incident response. |

### Quick‑Start Example

**FastAPI + Pydantic**

